WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.770
Film Courage: I saw you write something about
“Dull Bits” in a script and I’m curious

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:04.770
what that means?

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.810
Wendy Kram, owner of Wendy’s LA4hire: Yes.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:11.360
[Alfred] Hitchcock has one of my favorite
quotes and let’s see for all of those who

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.250
are Hitchcock aficionados forgive me if I
don’t have it exactly right but it’s something

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:25.080
to the effect of “Movies are exactly like
real life but with all the dull bits cut out.”

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.390
And essentially if you think about films you
are lying.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.390
Movies are lies.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.989
So let’s say you’re doing a story about
about Helen Keller or Abraham Lincoln.

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:44.620
Let’s say the span where it takes place
over a year or takes place over a lifetime

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.129
or twenty years.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:51.749
We’re seeing a 2-hour movie generally speaking
(an hour and a half, two hours, a little more).

00:00:51.749 --> 00:00:58.059
It’s a consolidation of real life so you
have to cut out the dull bits.

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:08.790
This is also a common mistake I see with writers
is the need to explain the entrance into a

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.220
scene and leaving.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:17.579
We don’t need to see characters walking
into a door, walking down the hall way, unless

00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:22.360
of course it’s very purposeful, you’re
doing a thriller and someone is walking down

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:28.710
a hall way and there may be someone in another
room that they don’t know about (it’s

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.440
very purposeful).

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.130
But we don’t need to see another character
entering and saying “Hi!

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.560
Oh, I just came in from the grocery store.”

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.100
We don’t need to play that scene out and
then see the character say “Okay, I’m

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.100
leaving now.”

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:49.520
I’m exaggerating a bit but there have been
scripts that I’ve read where every scene

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.479
practically starts with the character entering
and existing.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:02:02.450
Another example would be if we see a husband
and wife and they are heading to the in-laws.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:12.010
We don’t have to see them get in the car,
put their kids in the little seatbelt, baby

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:20.620
seat, drive the full distance (obviously)
or even part of the distance and we see them

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.540
get out of the driveway, walk up the door,
ring the doorbell.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.760
So that’s an example of you cut out deliberately
those dull bits.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.489
Law &amp; Order will sometimes say to writers
“Think of the Law &amp; Order edits.”

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:45.650
So you may have a scene where one of the detective
says “So do you know about…have you ever

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.400
seen this victim?”

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.400
“You know what?

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.750
The last time I saw her she was partying with
her friend Jessie.”

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.260
Where do you find Jessie?

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.879
“She’s always hanging out at such-and-such
club.”

00:02:57.879 --> 00:02:58.879
Boom!

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:04.799
We’re at the club and sometimes we might
pick up a scene mid conversation.

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:07.769
So that’s one example of taking out dull
bits.

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:10.170
Film Courage: That’s a great analogy, I
like that.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.659
Do you think people get some of that confused
with novel writing?

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:15.140
Wendy: Yes.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:23.760
And even in novels you don’t always have
characters entering and existing but with

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:30.129
novel writing one of the things you can do
is get fully inside the head of your character.

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:37.370
So if she’s walking down the street and
she sees a leaf and it reminds her of when

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:44.160
she was young and (this is in Faulkner) and
lost her virginity.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:52.060
We don’t have the luxury in scripts, you
can do of course a fantasy sequence or a flashback

00:03:52.060 --> 00:04:01.950
but generally speaking you can’t go on and
you can’t just like in book describe smells.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.989
In a movie you can’t really describe smell.

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:12.750
You can say that a character burns toast and
there is smoke coming out of the toaster,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.500
so definitely it has to be much more economical.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.610
One of the things I’ve said a lot (I was
an English major), one of my greatest teachers

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:31.270
was Shakespeare and when I say that of course
I had wonderful teachers who were guides but

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:38.100
one of the greatest teachers is reading great
authors, great screenwriters, great playwrights.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:45.860
I talk about a lot of times with writers giving
them feedback about not writing with exposition.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:54.880
Harold Pinter is a master at subtext, I recommend
Matthew Weiner (MAD MEN).

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.910
Characters rarely say exactly what they’re
feeling, there is so much subtext and it just

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.139
makes the writing that much more powerful.

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:08.009
Film Courage: Do you think the lost art of
novel reading will come back?

00:05:08.009 --> 00:05:14.680
Wendy: I don’t know if I feel it’s that
lost even though Barnes and Noble is sadly

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:22.250
closing but I think there are just so many
wonderful books out there and I know so many

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.080
people who are in book clubs and J.K.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.840
Rowling, bless her!

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.600
She is responsible for getting more people
(more children) into reading and there are

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.100
so many great authors out there and of course
as we know books are a great, great source

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.919
for movies.

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:46.490
Hollywood loves intellectual properties, they
like source material.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:53.380
Another strategy sometimes with screenwriters
they have a script and it’s well-written

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.870
and they’ve been having a hard time for
whatever reason gaining traction.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:03.750
Sometimes they may turn it into a book and
if the book starts to get traction then they

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:09.190
sort of reverse engineer, go back and then
sell the script.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:11.539
Sell the book and the script based on the
book.

